steps.body:
Go has no `condition ? a : b` operator, and `if` is a statement, not an expression, so `label := if x { ... }` does not compile either. Instead, declare the variable first, then assign it in each branch:

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	n := 14
	{{code}}
	fmt.Println(n, "is", label)
}
```

```go
var label string
if n%2 == 0 {
	label = "even"
} else {
	label = "odd"
}
```

You already used this shape for the letter grade chain in the if-else lesson. It is the standard replacement for a ternary. `var label string` creates the variable with its zero value `""`, and each branch replaces it. Use `=` inside the branches, not `:=`. Writing `label := "even"` inside the braces declares a brand new `label` that hides the outer one and disappears at the closing brace. Here the compiler catches it as `label declared but not used`, but if the branch also used the inner `label`, the program would compile and the outer `label` would silently stay `""`.

Assigning a string is cheap: Go copies a small header (a pointer to the text and its length), never the characters themselves.

example.code:
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	n := 14
	var parity string
	if n%2 == 0 {
		parity = "even"
	} else {
		parity = "odd"
	}
	fmt.Println(n, "is", parity)

	count := 1
	var word string
	if count == 1 {
		word = "item"
	} else {
		word = "items"
	}
	fmt.Println(count, word)
}

